WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.062
Here's a way to see all three angles.

00:00:03.062 --> 00:00:09.500
Start with the orbit lying flat in the equator, with its periapsis pointing straight along I.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:18.080
Turn one: spin the whole orbit about K by an angle, capital Omega.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:23.369
The direction where the periapsis now points is about to become a hinge.

00:00:23.369 --> 00:00:25.440
It's called the line of nodes.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:33.500
Capital Omega is measured in the equator, from I, counterclockwise as seen from the North Pole.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.921
It can be anything from zero to three hundred sixty degrees.

00:00:37.921 --> 00:00:41.900
Its name is the right ascension of the ascending node.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:47.900
Turn two: tilt the orbit about the line of nodes, by the inclination, i.

00:00:48.267 --> 00:00:52.659
Now the orbit plane and the equator cross along the line of nodes.

00:00:52.659 --> 00:00:55.987
Each lap, the satellite crosses the equator twice.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.954
Where it crosses heading north is the ascending node.

00:00:59.954 --> 00:01:03.576
Where it crosses heading south is the descending node.

00:01:03.576 --> 00:01:07.600
Now you can see why Omega is measured to the ascending node.

00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:16.013
The inclination is also the angle between K and the angular momentum vector h, which sticks straight out of the orbit plane.

00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:21.727
Inclination only runs from zero to one hundred eighty degrees.

00:01:22.067 --> 00:01:27.174
Below ninety, the satellite travels eastward, the same way the Earth spins.

00:01:27.174 --> 00:01:28.808
That's a prograde orbit.

00:01:28.808 --> 00:01:32.894
At exactly ninety, it passes over both poles: a polar orbit.

00:01:32.894 --> 00:01:36.027
Above ninety, it travels westward: retrograde.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:43.600
So i says how steeply the plane is tilted, and Omega says which way the tilt faces.

00:01:44.566 --> 00:01:48.207
Turn three happens inside the tilted plane.

00:01:48.207 --> 00:01:53.966
Spin the ellipse about h by the argument of periapsis, little omega.

00:01:55.266 --> 00:02:03.226
So little omega is the angle from the ascending node to the periapsis, measured in the orbit plane, in the direction the satellite moves.

00:02:03.566 --> 00:02:05.803
The ellipse is now completely placed.

00:02:05.803 --> 00:02:13.846
The last number, the true anomaly at epoch theta zero, is the angle from periapsis to the satellite at the moment of the measurement.

00:02:15.266 --> 00:02:20.651
Notice the order of the turns: about K, then about the node line, then about h.

00:02:20.651 --> 00:02:22.560
Three, then one, then three.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:26.786
Keep that pattern in mind; it comes back as a rotation matrix.

00:02:28.266 --> 00:02:32.186
Here's the Molniya orbit from the beginning, built the same way.

00:02:33.133 --> 00:02:39.730
Little omega of two seventy puts the perigee at the southernmost point of the orbit.

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:48.133
So the long, slow apogee half hangs high over the northern hemisphere, where it can watch Russia for hours.

00:02:49.066 --> 00:02:50.786
Quick question before we move on.

00:02:54.466 --> 00:02:55.619
Still northward.

00:02:55.619 --> 00:02:59.438
The ascending node is always the northbound crossing.

00:02:59.438 --> 00:03:04.986
What changes is that the motion over the Earth runs westward instead of east.

00:03:05.899 --> 00:03:09.179
Two kinds of orbit break these definitions.

00:03:09.532 --> 00:03:15.134
A circular orbit has no periapsis, so little omega and theta have nothing to measure from.

00:03:15.134 --> 00:03:21.545
Instead we use the argument of latitude, u, measured from the ascending node straight to the satellite.

00:03:21.545 --> 00:03:24.532
For any orbit, u equals little omega plus theta.

00:03:24.866 --> 00:03:33.111
An equatorial orbit never crosses the equator, so it has no ascending node, and capital Omega is undefined.

00:03:33.111 --> 00:03:39.430
Then we use the longitude of periapsis, measured from I straight to the periapsis.

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:42.666
It equals capital Omega plus little omega.

00:03:43.066 --> 00:03:52.837
And the true longitude, capital Omega plus little omega plus theta, measures from I all the way to the satellite.

00:03:52.837 --> 00:03:54.306
It always exists.

00:03:55.299 --> 00:03:58.419
That's every orbital element and what it looks like.
